// The Barrows: the six mounds, the six crypts, and the state of a run.
//
// WHY THERE ARE COORDINATES IN HERE AT ALL. The six mounds are TERRAIN, not locs - Old School has
// you dig into them with a spade and there is no staircase to click - so the only way a script can
// know where a mound is, is to be told. They were not guessed: the mounds are the six local maxima
// of the heightmap in m55_51, measured off the map (claude/barrows-survey.md has the measurement),
// and each one is paired with a brother by the compass direction the wiki gives his crypt. Five
// directions and a centre against five outer peaks and one central one, so the pairing is forced
// rather than chosen:
//
//     Ahrim   centre       (3566, 3289) h=85    the highest and the most central, as the wiki says
//     Dharok  north-east   (3576, 3299) h=76
//     Guthan  south-east   (3578, 3283) h=77
//     Karil   south        (3567, 3277) h=74
//     Torag   south-west   (3554, 3284) h=75
//     Verac   north-west   (3558, 3298) h=79
^barrows_mound_ahrim = 0_55_51_46_25
^barrows_mound_dharok = 0_55_51_56_35
^barrows_mound_guthan = 0_55_51_58_19
^barrows_mound_karil = 0_55_51_47_13
^barrows_mound_torag = 0_55_51_34_20
^barrows_mound_verac = 0_55_51_38_34

// How close to a peak counts as standing on the mound. The mound tops measure three to seven tiles
// across at the height that makes them peaks, so two tiles either side of the centre is the top of
// one and not its slope.
^barrows_mound_radius = 2

// WHERE A DIG PUTS YOU, and it is not under the mound. The crypts are a compact layout of their own
// at level 3 of m55_151 and they sit nowhere near their mounds, so each dig goes to the named crypt
// rather than to the coordinate below the hole. Each of these is the free tile beside that crypt's
// staircase which is nearest its sarcophagus - the inside of the room, derived from the map's own
// occupancy rather than picked by eye, because the six staircases have four different rotations
// between them and "the tile to the south" is a different side for each.
^barrows_crypt_ahrim = 3_55_151_37_39
^barrows_crypt_dharok = 3_55_151_36_54
^barrows_crypt_guthan = 3_55_151_14_40
^barrows_crypt_karil = 3_55_151_26_20
^barrows_crypt_torag = 3_55_151_45_20
^barrows_crypt_verac = 3_55_151_57_39

// HOW LONG A WOKEN BROTHER LASTS if nobody finishes him. Old School ties him to the crypt you are
// standing in and forgets him when you leave; there is no instance here, so he needs a lifetime, and
// the quest_100 bosses' ^hundred_boss_lifetime = 1500 is the nearest precedent - fifteen minutes,
// which is twenty times a fight and still a floor that cleans itself. A brother who times out leaves
// his bit UNSET, so his sarcophagus is still full: he escaped rather than died, which is the honest
// outcome for a player who dug him up and ran.
^barrows_brother_life = 1500

// One bit of %barrows_killed per brother: set when he dies, so his sarcophagus is empty for the
// rest of the run. The run itself is cleared by looting the chest, which is the other half of this
// round - until then the bits persist, which is the safe direction to be wrong in: a sarcophagus
// that says it is empty is a puzzle, one that hands out a second Dharok is a farm.
^barrows_bit_ahrim = 0
^barrows_bit_dharok = 1
^barrows_bit_guthan = 2
^barrows_bit_karil = 3
^barrows_bit_torag = 4
^barrows_bit_verac = 5
^barrows_brothers = 6

// =============================================================================================
// THE TUNNELS
// =============================================================================================
// WHAT WAS ALREADY IN THE CACHE, and the reason almost none of this needed inventing. The Barrows
// run is modelled in varbits that 377 already ships, and every loc down there is already a
// multiloc reading one of them:
//
//   %barrows_kills  bits 0-5   barrows_killed_ahrim .. barrows_killed_verac
//                   bits 6-15  barrows_killed_monster - ten bits, which is exactly 0..1000
//                   bit  16    barrows_chest_open     - the chest's own multivar
//                   bits 17-31 barrows_killed_count
//   %barrows        bits 0-2   free, and now barrows_entry_crypt (barrows.varbit)
//                   bits 6-9   barrows_chamber_a/c/g/i - each one a ladder's multivar
//                   bits 10-25 barrows_door_a .. barrows_door_p - each a door pair's multivar,
//                              0 = the unlocked form with Open on it, 1 = the locked form
//                   bits 29-31 barrows_puzzle - unused here, see claude/barrows-tunnels.md
//
// So there is nothing to add to the packs for any of it, and the client already knows how to draw
// a locked door, a missing ladder and an open chest.

// The whole tunnel level, used to ask "did this die down there?" of an npc's own coordinate.
^barrows_tunnel_sw = 0_55_151_0_0
^barrows_tunnel_ne = 0_55_151_63_63

// And the crypts, which are the SAME map square one level up - m55_151 holds both, the six crypts
// at level 3 and the tunnels at level 0, with nothing in between. The prayer drain asks about both.
^barrows_crypt_sw = 3_55_151_0_0
^barrows_crypt_ne = 3_55_151_63_63

// WHICH CRYPT IS THE WAY IN. Old School picks one of the six per run - "searching a sarcophagus in
// one of the six crypts randomly leads to a passage" - and that brother then turns up when the
// chest is looted rather than out of his own box. %barrows_entry_crypt holds his bit PLUS ONE so
// that 0 can mean "no run in progress", which is also what the chest resets it to.
^barrows_entry_none = 0

// THE FOUR CHAMBERS WITH A LADDER, and the tile the passage drops you on. There are six crypts and
// only four ladders in the map, so the chamber cannot be derived from the crypt: it is rolled on
// the way in and remembered by its own multivar, which is the same bit that makes its ladder exist.
// Each tile is the ladder's neighbour on the side facing the middle of the tunnel - the ladder's
// own tile is flagged blocked on the map, which is why the drop is beside it and not on it.
^barrows_chambers = 4
^barrows_chamber_a = 0
^barrows_chamber_c = 1
^barrows_chamber_g = 2
^barrows_chamber_i = 3
^barrows_chamber_tile_a = 0_55_151_15_48
^barrows_chamber_tile_c = 0_55_151_47_48
^barrows_chamber_tile_g = 0_55_151_15_14
^barrows_chamber_tile_i = 0_55_151_47_14

// The maze table in barrows.enum. Every row keeps all four ladders walking distance from the chest,
// which tools/barrowsmaze.py proves against the map and the battery re-proves on every run.
^barrows_mazes = 24
^barrows_door_first = 10
^barrows_door_last = 25

// WHAT A DOOR LETS OUT, out of 128, from the wiki: 12 a brother you have not killed, 52 a skeleton,
// 32 a bloodworm, 32 a crypt rat. Written as the cumulative edges of those four bands.
^barrows_spawn_denom = 128
^barrows_spawn_brother = 12
^barrows_spawn_skeleton = 64
^barrows_spawn_bloodworm = 96
// "When opening a door to a room that already contains eleven enemies or more, no new monster will
// be spawned" - so this is a ceiling on what is already there, not on what is added.
^barrows_spawn_crowd = 11
^barrows_spawn_range = 7

// REWARD POTENTIAL. The combat level of everything killed in the tunnels goes into a pool capped at
// 1000; each brother killed adds 2 on top, which is where 1012 comes from. The brothers themselves
// do NOT pay their combat level - the cap arithmetic is what says so - so the pool skips them.
^barrows_potential_cap = 1000
^barrows_potential_brother = 2
^barrows_potential_max = 1012

// THE CHEST. One roll, plus one per brother killed, to a ceiling of seven; and per roll a piece of
// Barrows equipment at 1/(450 - 58 * brothers), which is 1/392 at one brother and 1/102 at six.
^barrows_rolls_base = 1
^barrows_rolls_max = 7
^barrows_equip_base = 450
^barrows_equip_step = 58
^barrows_equip_pieces = 4

// THE BANDS. A roll is a value in 1..potential, and the reward is the last band the roll reached,
// which is why each number below is both "the potential this item needs" and "where its band
// starts". 380 + 125*5 + 6 + 1 = 1012 exactly. tools/barrowschestspec.json quotes the wiki.
^barrows_rp_mind = 381
^barrows_rp_chaos = 506
^barrows_rp_death = 631
^barrows_rp_blood = 756
^barrows_rp_boltrack = 881
^barrows_rp_keyhalf = 1006
^barrows_rp_dragonmed = 1012
^barrows_loot_coins_low = 2
^barrows_loot_coins_high = 774
^barrows_loot_mind_low = 253
^barrows_loot_mind_high = 336
^barrows_loot_chaos_low = 112
^barrows_loot_chaos_high = 139
^barrows_loot_death_low = 70
^barrows_loot_death_high = 83
^barrows_loot_blood_low = 37
^barrows_loot_blood_high = 43
^barrows_loot_boltrack_low = 35
^barrows_loot_boltrack_high = 40

// COREY'S ADDITION, and the one thing in this file Old School does not have: a Barrows teleport tab
// at 1/10 on a looted chest, four to six at a time, landing on the tile he asked for.
^barrows_tele_rate = 10
^barrows_tele_low = 4
^barrows_tele_high = 6
^barrows_tele_dest = 0_55_51_45_48

// =============================================================================================
// THE BROTHERS' OWN SET EFFECTS, AND THE PRAYER DRAIN
// =============================================================================================
// EVERY ONE OF THE SIX HAS AN EFFECT OF HIS OWN, which the first version of this content got
// wrong twice over: it said Dharok's 57 belonged to the armour and not to him, and it gave Ahrim
// and Karil the melee AI that every npc gets by default, so the mage walked up and swung a
// quarterstaff. The wiki's own words, one brother at a time, are in tools/barrowsspec.json.
//
// THE CACHE ALREADY HAD THE ART FOR THE FOUR THAT NEED ANY: barrows_ahirm_blighted_aura (its
// spelling, not mine), barrows_karil_tainted_shot, barrows_guthan_effect and barrows_torag_effect
// are spotanims that nothing was using. Dharok's and Verac's effects are numbers, not pictures.
^barrows_ahrim_effect_pct = 20
^barrows_ahrim_strength_drain = 5
^barrows_guthan_effect_pct = 25
^barrows_karil_effect_pct = 25
^barrows_karil_agility_pct = 20
^barrows_torag_effect_pct = 25
^barrows_torag_energy_pct = 20
^barrows_verac_pierce_pct = 25

// DHAROK'S WRETCHED STRENGTH is +1% of his max hit for every hitpoint he is MISSING, which takes
// him from 29 to 57 at one hitpoint - and 29 is what this engine's own max-hit formula gives from
// strength 100 and a bonus of 105, so both ends of the wiki's "29 (57 at 1 Hitpoints)" fall out of
// numbers that were already in his record. There is no constant to write for it.

// AHRIM CASTS, and what he casts is Iban's Blast: the cache has no projectile of Ahrim's own, and
// of everything in the spell table it is the one whose casting, travel and impact graphics are the
// dark purple burst his attack actually looks like. The max hit is FORCED to the wiki's 20 rather
// than derived, because a magic max hit off his magic level alone is 11 - mage_arena_2.rs2 forces
// its champions' the same way and for the same reason.
^barrows_ahrim_maxhit = 20
// He also throws Confuse, Weaken and Curse, which the wiki lists and which are real spells here,
// so the debuffs land through the engine's own path. One cast in four is one of the three.
^barrows_ahrim_debuff_odds = 4

// KARIL'S +55 RANGED STRENGTH HAS A HOME AFTER ALL. The first version said this engine had no npc
// param for it; param=rangebonus is exactly it, read by ~npc_ranged_maxhit, and 55 through that
// formula gives a max hit of 20 - the wiki's number for him, from the wiki's own bonus. It was
// only ever missing.

// THE PRAYER DRAIN. A brother's face appears every 18 seconds underground and takes eight points,
// plus one for every brother already killed, so the last of them costs fourteen. 18 seconds is 30
// ticks. The face itself is not drawn: barrows_chathead_fade is in the cache for it, but putting a
// chathead on screen means opening a dialogue interface, and a dialogue that opens itself every
// thirty ticks in the middle of a fight is worse than no picture.
^barrows_drain_interval = 30
^barrows_drain_base = 8
